{
  "id": "verbpraefixe",
  "name": "Verb prefixes: over-, under-, out-, re-, mis-",
  "level": "C1",
  "status": "geprueft",
  "subtitle": "A prefix changes the meaning of a verb. over- means too much, under- too little, out- better than, re- again, mis- wrongly: overcook, underestimate, outlive, rewrite, misunderstand.",
  "briefMarks": [
    "over-",
    "under-",
    "out-",
    "re-",
    "mis-"
  ],
  "source": "icanspeak English original English text (grammar-c1 run, 2026-10-29)",
  "concept": {
    "flow": [
      {
        "text": "prefix: over- · under- · out- · re- · mis-",
        "variant": "prep"
      },
      {
        "text": "+ verb",
        "variant": "verb"
      },
      {
        "text": "= new meaning"
      }
    ],
    "exampleHtml": "<span class=\"vp-prep\">over</span><span class=\"vp-verb\">cook</span> = cook for too long. — <span class=\"vp-prep\">mis</span><span class=\"vp-verb\">understand</span> = understand wrongly. — <span class=\"vp-prep\">re</span><span class=\"vp-verb\">write</span> = write again.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "<strong>over- and under-</strong> show <strong>too much</strong> and <strong>too little</strong>. <em>overcook, oversleep, overestimate, overreact</em> (do or think too much). <em>undercook, underestimate, underpay, underuse</em> (do or think too little). <em>I overslept. She underestimated the exam.</em>",
        "tag": "over- / under-"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>out-</strong> means <strong>do something better, longer or in greater numbers</strong> than someone else. <em>outnumber, outlive, outperform, outrun, outgrow.</em> <em>Our team was outnumbered. She outlived her husband by ten years.</em> <em>outgrow</em> is special: you grow too big for something (<em>He has outgrown his shoes.</em>).",
        "tag": "out-"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>re-</strong> means <strong>again</strong>: <em>rewrite, rethink, reapply, restart, rebuild.</em> <em>Could you rewrite the first paragraph?</em> <strong>mis-</strong> means <strong>wrongly or badly</strong>: <em>misunderstand, misread, mispronounce, misplace, misspell.</em> <em>I misread the sign.</em>",
        "tag": "re- / mis-"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>un- and dis-</strong> reverse an action: <em>undo, unpack, unlock, disagree, disconnect.</em> <em>She unpacked her bag. I disagree with you.</em> <strong>Stress:</strong> in verbs the stress is usually on the root, not on the prefix: <em>overCOOK, misunderSTAND, reWRITE, outNUMber.</em>",
        "tag": "un- / dis- and stress"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I have misunderstand you.</em> → ✓ <em>I have misunderstood you.</em> (the past forms follow the base verb: understand, understood) · ✗ <em>I over-slept this morning.</em> → ✓ <em>I overslept.</em> (no hyphen in over-, under-, out-, mis- verbs) · ✗ <em>Please rewrite it again.</em> → ✓ <em>Please rewrite it.</em> (re- already means again)",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "You cannot add a prefix to every verb: ✗ <em>outsleep</em>. If you are not sure that a verb exists, check a dictionary. The same prefixes also make nouns and adjectives: <em>overtime, a misunderstanding, underpaid staff, an overcooked egg</em> (see <a href=\"/noun-suffixes\">noun suffixes and word building</a> and <a href=\"/ed-ing-adjectives\">-ed and -ing adjectives</a>). Two more prefixes you will meet: <strong>co-</strong> = together (<em>coexist, cooperate</em>) and <strong>pre-</strong> = before (<em>preheat, prepay</em>). <strong>Spelling:</strong> the prefix is written solid, but a hyphen can show a different meaning or avoid a clash of vowels: <em>recover</em> (get better) and <em>re-cover</em> (cover again); <em>resign</em> (leave a job) and <em>re-sign</em> (sign again); <em>re-enter</em>, <em>re-elect</em>.",
